>
  <p>
    New York Assembly bill A11635 would require anyone advertising a home for sale or lease
    with a digitally altered or AI-generated photo to put a statement on or next to each
    altered image saying it has been altered. For ads posted online, it would also require
    the unaltered version, or a link to it. It is a bill, not a law: it was introduced on
    July 29, 2026 and sent to the Assembly Housing Committee. If passed, it takes effect 60
    days later.
  </p>

  <h2>What the bill would require</h2>
  <p>It would add a new section 443-b to the Real Property Law:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A statement</strong> "disclosing that the image has been altered,"
      "conspicuously located on or adjacent to each altered image," in any advertisement or
      promotional material for the sale or lease of real property.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The original, online.</strong> When the ad is posted on a website, the
      unaltered versions of the images must be included in the posting. A link to the
      original image also satisfies this.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who would be covered</h2>
  <p>
    The bill names "a real estate broker, seller, property owner or property manager." That
    is broader than California's sales law, which covers only licensees and people acting
    for them, and it reaches owners who list without an agent.
  </p>

  <h2>What counts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Digitally altered image</strong>: a still photo altered with photo-editing
      software or AI to add, remove or change elements of the property, "including, but not
      limited to, furniture, appliances, flooring, and walls."
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>AI-generated image</strong>: a still photo or video "created in whole or in
      part by generative artificial intelligence."
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not covered</strong>: lighting, sharpening, white balance, color correction,
      angle, straightening, cropping, exposure, and other common adjustments "that do not
      change the conditions of the real property."
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it compares with California</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sales and leases from the start. California covered rentals only with a second law, AB 2025, from 2027.</li>
    <li>Explicit coverage of AI-generated video. California's laws speak of images.</li>
    <li>The link to the original is one option for website postings. California's sales law requires a link, URL or QR code in every ad, print included.</li>
    <li>Its list of example elements is shorter, but "including, but not limited to" leaves it open.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If you list in New York</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing is required by this bill yet. Labeling staged and AI-edited photos and keeping
    originals available costs little, and it is what the bill, California's laws and most
    MLS rules converge on. The sponsor's memo argues that renters and buyers "deserve a
    clear understanding of the current condition of the home or the property, not simply
    its potential."
  </p>

